#!/usr/bin/env nu
# skill-mirror — RETIRED 2026-07-29. Do not reinstate.
#
# WHY IT IS GONE
# --------------
# It copied ~/.claude/skills/ → ~/Assistants/claude-skills/ on the premise that
# the Claude directory was authoritative and the Assistants copy was a derived
# mirror for Codex. That premise stopped being true a long time ago:
# ~/.claude/skills is a SYMLINK to the shared directory, so source and target
# were already the same files and the script was a no-op.
#
# After the 2026-07-29 vendor-neutral migration it became actively harmful:
# ~/Assistants/claude-skills/ no longer exists (it is now ~/Assistants/skills/),
# so this script's `mkdir $target` would RECREATE the vendor-named directory and
# populate it — producing exactly the second live tree the design decision
# forbids, and silently splitting the source of truth.
#
# THE MODEL NOW
# -------------
#   ~/Assistants/skills/        canonical, vendor-neutral, the ONLY editable source
#   ~/.claude/skills   -> symlink to it   (harness exposure, never a source)
#   ~/.codex/skills/<name> -> per-skill links (Codex owns .system/, so no dir link)
#
# Content flows OUT of ~/Assistants/skills/. No vendor directory is a source and
# no adapter writes back. Editing a skill needs NO mirror step — you are already
# editing the canonical file. Syncthing carries it to the other machine.
#
# Decision record: Assistants/shared/design-forum/meta/2026-07-12-vendor-neutral-skill-directory.md
# Migration record: Assistants/shared/ASSISTANT-HANDOFF.md (2026-07-29)

def main [] {
    print "skill-mirror is RETIRED (2026-07-29) and does nothing."
    print ""
    print "There is no mirror to maintain. ~/Assistants/skills/ is the canonical"
    print "source; ~/.claude/skills is a symlink to it. Editing a skill file IS"
    print "editing the canonical copy — no sync step is required."
    print ""
    print "If you reached here from a checklist, that checklist is stale."
    print "See: Assistants/shared/SKILL-INFRASTRUCTURE.md"
    exit 1
}
